\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Energy Audit of the Studio Kiln}
\author{M.~Reyes}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Background}
The studio replaced its thirty-year-old electric kiln in
March, and we have now logged twelve consecutive firings on
the new unit. Both kilns served the same bisque-and-glaze
schedule, so the comparison is about as clean as a working
pottery can make it.

\section{Findings}
The new kiln reduces energy use by 50\%. Insulation accounts
for most of the gain: the skin temperature at full power
dropped by 30\%, and the cooling phase now holds above 200
degrees for two hours longer. Peak demand is unchanged,
because the elements still draw full power while ramping,
but the duty cycle fell from 70\% of the firing to just
under half, and it is the duty cycle that drives the bill.

\section{Recommendation}
At current tariffs the unit pays for itself in four years.
We recommend fitting the same controller to the small test
kiln, which should capture roughly the same proportional
saving on a smaller base.
\end{document}

